.TH RMU 1 "2026-09-08" "rmu 0.7.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rmu \- move files into the FreeDesktop Trash with GNU rm-compatible arguments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rmu
.RI [ OPTION ]...\ [ FILE ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rmu
accepts GNU
.BR rm (1)
arguments but, by default, moves each top-level operand into the FreeDesktop
Trash used by Linux desktop environments.  Files remain recoverable through
the desktop or
.BR rmuctl (1).
.PP
.B \-\-delegate
or
.B RMU_MODE=delegate
selects exact GNU delegation. RMU removes its own mode selectors, verifies
a GNU coreutils
.BR rm ,
and replaces itself with that executable. GNU rm therefore owns parsing,
prompts, traversal, diagnostics, signals, and exit status in delegate mode.
.PP
There is one deliberate exception:
.B \-d
permanently removes an empty directory with the kernel's atomic directory
removal operation, as GNU rm does.  Use
.B \-r
if the directory itself should remain recoverable.
.PP
No Trash-mode failure falls back to permanent deletion.  A compatible Trash
must be on the same mount as the operand so that publication can use an atomic
rename.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent operands and a missing operand, and never prompt.  Like GNU
rm, a later
.B \-i
or
.B \-I
can change the prompt policy.
.TP
.B \-i
Prompt before each top-level operand.
.TP
.B \-I
Prompt once before processing more than three operands, or before a recursive
operation.
.TP
.BR \-\-interactive [= WHEN ]
Set the prompt policy to
.BR never ,
.BR once ,
or
.BR always .
Without a value, use
.BR always .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
Permanently remove an empty directory.  A nonempty directory is refused unless
.B \-r
is also present.
.TP
.B \-\-one-file-system
Refuse a directory operand as a whole if it contains a nested mount.  rmu
does not create a partially trashed tree.
.TP
.B \-\-no-preserve-root
Disable the GNU rm root policy.  The filesystem root still cannot be moved
into Trash.  As with GNU rm, this option may not be abbreviated.
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve-root [= all ]
Do not remove
.BR / .
With
.BR all ,
also reject an operand on a different filesystem from its parent.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Allow directory operands.  Each accepted top-level directory is moved as one
recoverable item rather than unlinked descendant by descendant.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Explain successful actions.
.TP
.B \-\-trash
Explicitly select the default recoverable Trash mode. The last mode selector
wins and command-line mode selection overrides
.BR RMU_MODE .
.TP
.B \-\-delegate
Delegate every remaining argument to verified GNU coreutils
.BR rm .
RMU's Trash and progress enhancements do not apply.
.TP
.BR \-\-rmu-progress [= WHEN ]
Show tree totals, item and byte rates, an ETA when exact totals are available,
move status, and a final summary.  WHEN is
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never ;
the option without a value means
.BR auto .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RMU_MODE
Select the default operation mode:
.B trash
or
.BR delegate .
Trash is the default, and an explicit mode option takes precedence. Because
.B delegate
hands every affected invocation to destructive GNU rm, prefer the visible
command-line option unless persistent delegation is genuinely intended.
.TP
.B RMU_GNU_RM
Override the GNU coreutils
.B rm
executable used by delegate mode and the differential tests. A non-GNU
implementation and a path resolving back to RMU are refused.
.TP
.B RMU_PROGRESS
Default progress policy:
.BR never ,
.BR auto ,
or
.BR always .
An explicit command-line option takes precedence. In Trash mode, no environment
variable can enable force or change confirmation policy.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When present, stop option parsing at the first operand, matching GNU rm's
environment-sensitive ordering.  A later option-looking word is an operand.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Selects the home-filesystem Trash as
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash .
.TP
.B HOME
When
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset, the home-filesystem Trash is
.IR $HOME/.local/share/Trash .
.SH TRASH LOCATIONS
For an operand on the home filesystem, rmu uses the XDG data Trash.  On
another mounted filesystem it uses a valid shared
.IR .Trash/UID
directory or the private
.IR .Trash-UID
fallback required by the FreeDesktop Trash Specification.  Existing Trash
directories are validated before use.
.PP
Each payload has a matching
.I .trashinfo
record containing the encoded original path and local deletion time.  Directory
sizes are recorded in the optional FreeDesktop
.I directorysizes
cache.
.SH PROGRESS AND SUMMARIES
Progress performs an additional counting pass over directory trees.  Exact
totals and ETA are shown only when the entire tree is readable.  Otherwise
counts are labelled as lower bounds and the summary is marked incomplete.
Allocated bytes are reported, hard-linked inodes are counted once, and the
final summary distinguishes failed operands from skipped operands.
.PP
The scanner requires a mounted Linux
.I /proc
filesystem.  If scanning is unavailable, the top-level Trash move can still
succeed, but optional size and progress information can be incomplete.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested operations succeeded, or force made a missing operand ignorable.
.TP
.B 1
At least one operand failed or was refused, or the command line or environment
setting was invalid.
.PP
Delegate mode returns GNU rm's exit status because RMU replaces itself with
that process.
.PP
In Trash mode, a non-interactive output failure is recorded while later
operands continue to be processed; the command then exits nonzero.  This keeps
requested recoverable moves from being abandoned when an output pipe closes.
GNU rm may instead terminate on
.BR SIGPIPE .
Delegate mode retains GNU rm's behavior.
.SH NOTES
GNU rm recursively unlinks descendants.  rmu instead moves each top-level
directory as one Trash entry.  Per-descendant prompts and verbose output
therefore do not apply.
.PP
A trailing-slash symlink is refused without following it.  A source reached
through a separate bind mount of the home filesystem is refused until a safe
copy fallback exists.  Do not empty the desktop Trash concurrently with an
active rmu operation.
.PP
Trash-mode diagnostics and prompts are English.  Dynamically linked glibc
builds use the locale's affirmative-response expression; static musl releases
accept ASCII
.B y
or
.BR yes .
Delegate mode retains GNU rm's own localization and response handling.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B rmu file.txt
Move a file into the desktop Trash.
.TP
.B rmu --trash file.txt
Make the default recoverable mode explicit.
.TP
.B rmu --delegate -r build-directory
Replace RMU with GNU rm and permanently remove the directory using GNU's exact
behavior.
.TP
.B rmu -r --rmu-progress large-tree
Move a directory as one item while reporting scan and move progress.
.TP
.B rmu -d empty-directory
Permanently remove an empty directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR rmuctl (1)
.UR https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash/latest/
FreeDesktop Trash Specification 1.0
.UE
